{"author":{"id":"89da5fec8eff6d69dbcdd68f81be7e9163b16f34b17e978ecf3554487379f453"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1773941678,"nonce":"kuio5Z7cwbWcAY968Nx/X8p591M=","title":"Console hydration: use conversation history as authoritative base","message":"Redesign the hydration effect in TerminalPane.tsx to use conversation history (JSONL) as the authoritative base layer, with in-memory stream events as the live tail.\n\nCurrently, the hydration effect (deps: [providerSessionId, sessionId]) has a hasRenderableEvents gate: if in-memory stream events contain assistant events, it uses them directly and never consults conversation history. This means old SDK sessions missing user events show assistant-only content, even though conversation history (from JSONL files, always complete with user messages) is available.\n\nThe fix: make conversation history the primary source, with in-memory events providing only the \"live tail\" (events not yet persisted to JSONL).\n\nKey changes:\n1. Replace the hydration effect to fetch BOTH conversation history and in-memory events in parallel\n2. Add computeLiveTail helper to compute events not yet in history\n3. Add SSE buffering during hydration (hydratingRef/pendingSSERef) to prevent clobbering\n4. Remove hasRenderableEvents from the hydration gate (keep if used elsewhere)\n5. Keep the 20-second polling fallback (Bug: 2a04856) as-is","files":null}]}